1
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:12,380
is nothing more familiar, more mysterious, more breathtaking in its action, marvelous in its mechanics,

2
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:28,300
exquisite in its range of senses, and staggering in its ability to understand.

3
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:36,740
On a fantastic voyage through a single day, we plunge deep into the routine miracles of the human body.

4
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:46,080
Our instruments, engines, infrastructure, roadways and circuitry,

5
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:57,320
through 10,000 blinks, 20,000 breaths, 100,000 beats.

6
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:03,430
Hello.

7
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:04,430
Today is an ordinary, extraordinary day in the life of the incredible human machine.

8
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:28,120
Stardust is really all we are.

9
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:32,790
Oxygen, carbon, hydrogen, and a handful of elements that would cost very little at any chemical supply shop.

10
00:01:38,470 --> 00:01:40,130
But get these chemicals together,

11
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:44,140
marinate in a hospitable place for about 3.8 billion years,

12
00:01:44,810 --> 00:01:48,480
and the mundane mix of molecules becomes precious.

13
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:55,480
There are more than 6 billion human bodies living on Earth,

14
00:01:55,820 --> 00:02:01,490
and each of us is the amalgamation of some 100 trillion microscopic cells.

15
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:06,160
While the blueprint for each individual are 99.9% identical,

16
00:02:06,830 --> 00:02:09,500
no two of us are exactly the same.

17
00:02:13,170 --> 00:02:14,170
New Day dawns, each human machine begins the succession of

18
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:19,840
miracles that will take it from morning to midnight.

19
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:37,860
Muscles, bones, hearts, brains, all must marshal their forces and unite,

20
00:02:38,190 --> 00:02:38,860
just to wake us up.

21
00:02:52,870 --> 00:02:54,210
At the surface of it all, a velvety overcoat of cells and protein keeps us in, and the rest of the world out.

22
00:03:07,890 --> 00:03:11,560
Our radiator, our conduit of pain and pleasure, it is the body's largest organ, our skin.

23
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:22,900
Our skin, smooth and silky to the eyes and touch. A closer look presents a very different landscape.

24
00:03:27,910 --> 00:03:29,810
Magnified 600 times, our outermost skin is nothing but dead cells,

25
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:34,250
riddled with ridges and grooves, and pocked with countless bumps and holes.

26
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:45,930
Look closer still, and we find hundreds of thousands of bacteria inhabit every square inch of us.

27
00:03:55,940 --> 00:04:00,270
With every tick of the clock, our dead skin gets sloughed off.

28
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:05,610
We shed at least 600,000 particles of skin an hour, about a pound and a half's worth each year,

29
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:12,950
which accounts for as much as 80% of the dust in our houses.

30
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:26,630
But there's plenty of skin to go around.

31
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:30,300
If we could peel it off and lay it out flat, the average person's skin would cover some 18 square feet.

32
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:37,980
Though just millimeters thick, it would weigh about six pounds.

33
00:04:41,980 --> 00:04:44,990
And we're constantly making more.

34
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:46,320
Just wait a month or so, and you'll have a shiny new coat,

35
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:54,330
which means skin can't be all dead.

36
00:04:57,330 --> 00:04:58,330
Dip below the surface, and you find cells continuously dividing to replace those dead cells above.

37
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:10,340
Kilometers of blood vessels pulse to skin's connective tissues.

38
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:16,350
Not forgetting all those precious nerve endings.

39
00:05:22,020 --> 00:05:26,030
45 miles worth of nerve stretch from our heads down to our toes,

40
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:30,030
and many reach to our skin.

41
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:35,040
Some people have more nerve endings in some parts more than others.

42
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:38,710
If sensitivity were size dependent, we would look something like this.

43
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:49,380
Our super sensitive hands, feet, tongue and lips, each packed with touch receptors, would swell enormously.

44
00:05:53,390 --> 00:05:56,720
Our legs on the other hand would resemble a chickens.

45
00:05:59,730 --> 00:06:00,730
Good thing for us, there is more to this organ than touch.

46
00:06:05,070 --> 00:06:09,070
Skin is also our heating and cooling system.

47
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:13,410
And by helping maintain that comfy 98.6 degrees Fahrenheit,

48
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:16,410
it keeps us alive.

49
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:19,750
Through its network of blood vessels, skin carries as much as one-third of the heart's hot, freshly pumped blood.

50
00:06:27,090 --> 00:06:28,320
Get too hot, and these vessels can widen to release heat from our bodies.

51
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:36,100
But sometimes that's not enough.

52
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:47,680
A good workout can raise the body's temperature several degrees above normal.

53
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:48,780
A potentially deadly state if it weren't for skin's slick safety net.

54
00:06:55,780 --> 00:06:58,690
Sweat. More than 2 million holes across our skin can produce up to half a gallon of the liquid in an hour.

55
00:07:06,460 --> 00:07:07,460
The heated droplets evaporate into the air and leave the body cool.

56
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:14,470
A special camera that images heat, not light. We can see this air conditioning system at work.

57
00:07:22,810 --> 00:07:28,720
The hotter the body, the deeper the red.

58
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:31,150
No surprise, some of the warmest parts of our bodies, our foreheads, palms and armpits,

59
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:32,390
correlate with the greatest concentration of sweat glands.

60
00:07:48,170 --> 00:07:49,500
We're more likely to encounter the opposite extreme in our morning routine.

61
00:07:54,180 --> 00:07:55,510
Exposed to a chill, tiny muscles jerk our hairs to attention, bulging the skin around them.

62
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:05,850
Goose bumps.

63
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:12,860
Scientists have dubbed us the naked ape,

64
00:08:13,190 --> 00:08:17,530
but look closely, and we find we're anything but naked.

65
00:08:20,870 --> 00:08:24,210
Some of our skin cells form tubes that produce hair,

66
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:29,540
and we have as many of these follicles punctuating our skin as do our hairy relatives.

67
00:08:30,210 --> 00:08:32,880
some 5 million of them.

68
00:08:36,550 --> 00:08:38,220
Few hair cells divide.

69
00:08:38,550 --> 00:08:41,460
They push old ones up and out.

70
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:44,230
By the time our hair breaches the surface, it's dead,

71
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:47,460
which is why we don't feel pain with every haircut.

72
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:54,900
Cleaned and groomed, we venture into the bustling morning world.

73
00:09:01,910 --> 00:09:02,910
Around us, other incredible human machines greet the day

74
00:09:05,580 --> 00:09:09,580
in all different sizes and shapes, colors and textures.

75
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:17,160
But on some level, we all inhabit the same skin.

76
00:09:17,260 --> 00:09:21,260
Below the surface, each of us has about the same number of melanocytes,

77
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:25,600
the cells that pump the dark pigment melanin, into our hair and skin,

78
00:09:29,270 --> 00:09:37,610
It's just the amount of melanin these cells produce that determines whether we are black or white, brunette or blonde.

79
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:42,950
Generally speaking, the more melanin, the darker.

80
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:48,620
That same chemical adds color to a very different sensory organ.

81
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:50,290
organ, blue or brown, green or hazel.

82
00:09:53,290 --> 00:09:57,200
They are the most vital sensory organ that the incredible machine has.

83
00:09:59,630 --> 00:10:00,970
Our day has just begun, and the magnificent miracle of sight leads the way.

84
00:10:15,650 --> 00:10:18,650
In microseconds, our eyes sight, follow, focus, and process images fractions of an inch long,

85
00:10:21,660 --> 00:10:23,660
or moving at hundreds of miles per hour, enabling us to assess and appreciate the world

86
00:10:25,330 --> 00:10:37,330
more than any other sense.

87
00:10:39,010 --> 00:10:41,340
They may be the windows to our souls, but on a less poetic level,

88
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:46,680
eyes are just hungry harvesters of light, trapping and translating it into electrical impulses

89
00:10:47,010 --> 00:10:52,350
the brain can understand.

90
00:10:56,690 --> 00:10:59,360
Light hits the cornea first.

91
00:11:01,030 --> 00:11:01,360
This transparent layer, cleansed and lubricated about ten times a minute with every blink,

92
00:11:06,370 --> 00:11:10,370
admits and directs incoming light rays.

93
00:11:12,370 --> 00:11:15,040
There they pass through the dark opening of the pupil,

94
00:11:15,710 --> 00:11:15,940
then a transparent protein lens.

95
00:11:25,050 --> 00:11:25,290
Gatekeepers of light, the muscles of the colourful iris,

96
00:11:25,390 --> 00:11:28,720
squeeze the pupil closed against too much light.

97
00:11:29,390 --> 00:11:32,390
Not enough, the iris relaxes,

98
00:11:32,730 --> 00:11:34,060
and the pupil opens.

99
00:11:39,070 --> 00:11:41,070
And in just a fraction of a second, it can slide back and forth between the two.

100
00:11:44,740 --> 00:11:45,740
Focused by cornea and lens, light then flies through the jelly-like bulk of the eye

101
00:11:46,070 --> 00:11:57,320
and onto its rear wall. Just about a hundredth of an inch, thick.

102
00:11:57,420 --> 00:11:58,750
This is the retina, where more than 120 million photoreceptors convert light into electrical

103
00:11:59,420 --> 00:12:01,420
impulses before processing and shipping them off to the brain.

104
00:12:10,100 --> 00:12:10,330
Mind-boggling feat that soaks up about a third of our brain power, our brains continually

105
00:12:14,100 --> 00:12:20,440
compare new data with information processed a split second before.

106
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:23,440
Combined that with what they already expect to be there,

107
00:12:23,780 --> 00:12:27,020
and vision is born.

108
00:12:27,780 --> 00:12:32,450
At least that's how it's supposed to work.

109
00:12:32,790 --> 00:12:36,460
If it doesn't, the world can look more like this.

110
00:12:38,790 --> 00:12:40,130
If you would put your chin into the cup here, we're going to take a picture of...

111
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:44,130
62-year-old Linda Morford has a genetic disease called retinitis pigmentosa,

112
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:49,470
which has been gradually degrading her eyes photoreceptors for the past 40 years. Okay, that's good.

113
00:12:52,810 --> 00:12:55,040
Thank you. They haven't turned light into sight for the last ten.

114
00:12:55,140 --> 00:13:00,050
It's frustrating to lose your sight because you run into things, you run into people,

115
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:02,480
and it can be depressing.

116
00:13:02,820 --> 00:13:03,820
Just open up real long. Very good. Now, thanks to Dr. Mark Humayun of the University of Southern California, she may see again.

117
00:13:15,830 --> 00:13:17,500
All along we've been told this is impossible. It's science fiction. It can't happen. Good, look up.

118
00:13:18,170 --> 00:13:22,500
Humayun has implanted an ingenious little device at the back of Linda's eye.

119
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:26,170
Just 16 electrodes that should act as a simple retina, turning light into impulses that can be sent to the brain.

120
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:36,850
We lay it right on the retina. and the current stimulates the underlying nerve cells.

121
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:41,520
So when this information is received by the brain, you see a spot of light.

122
00:13:43,860 --> 00:13:45,190
To perceive those spots, Linda had to first wear a special pair of sunglasses that capture light,

123
00:13:49,860 --> 00:13:52,200
converted into electrical signals, and fire up the implants in her eyes.

124
00:14:01,540 --> 00:14:01,780
As the doctors activated the electrodes one by one, it started to work.

125
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:12,550
but Linda could now see light and movement. As they turn more and more electrodes on,

126
00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:16,890
I could see the lights on or the doorway.

127
00:14:17,230 --> 00:14:19,890
I could tell the difference between...

128
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:21,900
A working retina transmits.

129
00:14:33,910 --> 00:14:34,140
But with each passing day, her brain compensates

130
00:14:34,240 --> 00:14:34,480
and Linda sees more detail. What's over here? It's a house.

131
00:14:50,590 --> 00:14:50,930
We thought that 16 electrodes would never, ever give Linda or any of the patients the level of vision they have been able to attain.

132
00:14:55,930 --> 00:15:03,510
From simple flashes of light, our brains can somehow conjure meaningful images.

133
00:15:03,610 --> 00:15:04,940
After 10 years of blindness, Linda can see the grandchildren she never saw before.

134
00:15:10,280 --> 00:15:10,950
And they like to run in front of me. Where am I, Grandma? Where am I? I'm more connected to them, a little...

135
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:31,200
a little more part of their lives, you know. Even for those of us lucky enough to see 20/20,

136
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:37,970
the sense of sight does not work alone in the incredible human machine.

137
00:15:44,310 --> 00:15:47,980
On either side of our head are the body's microphones.

138
00:15:48,650 --> 00:15:49,990
riding a bike, landing, perfecting a dive,

139
00:15:53,990 --> 00:15:55,320
even taking a baby step,

140
00:15:55,660 --> 00:16:00,660
all would be impossible without the intricate gadgetry deep inside our ears.

141
00:16:06,670 --> 00:16:10,340
Three fluid-filled tubes work

142
00:16:10,670 --> 00:16:12,670
like carpenter's levels to help keep us balanced.

143
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:17,010
When we turn our heads, the fluids move, stimulating nerve cells,

144
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:22,350
and orienting the brain in three dimensions,

145
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:27,020
up down, left right, forward, backward.

146
00:16:29,020 --> 00:16:33,360
It's a powerful little mechanism that we can stimulate artificially.

147
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:38,030
Welcome to the weird world of tomorrow.

148
00:16:42,370 --> 00:16:43,270
With a special electrified headset, scientists in Japan have taken hold of our balance

149
00:16:43,370 --> 00:16:47,710
centers.

150
00:16:49,710 --> 00:16:53,050
By sending current down to those nerves in our inner ears, they've created remote-controlled human beings.

151
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:07,400
I've never experienced such a sensation. It was like being drunk on the deck of a boat rocking in the waves.

152
00:17:16,070 --> 00:17:16,410
The current is low voltage, just enough to throw people off balance and compel them to

153
00:17:18,070 --> 00:17:22,740
walk left, right,

154
00:17:23,080 --> 00:17:26,080
even trace the shape of a giant pretzel.

155
00:17:30,750 --> 00:17:32,320
My body was out of control.

156
00:17:33,090 --> 00:17:35,420
It was swaying to the left and to the right.

157
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:39,090
We can even remote control ourselves.

158
00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:44,330
We're not trying to control people.

159
00:17:44,430 --> 00:17:54,110
Or manipulate their actions. Rather, we want to help them, help guide them.

160
00:17:57,780 --> 00:18:00,120
Especially people with balance problems or dizziness.

161
00:18:07,460 --> 00:18:09,460
The researchers say one day it may even help navigational devices like GPS to physically guide us to our destinations or make a video game feel more like a roller coaster.

162
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:36,050
And our ears provide another powerful sensation to enrich our day.

163
00:18:44,160 --> 00:18:46,160
Every time we, or anything else for that matter, make a move or vibrate, we create ripples in the air.

164
00:18:46,830 --> 00:18:48,160
Distinct waves all traveling at different frequencies,

165
00:18:51,170 --> 00:18:53,500
which waft into our ears at some 750 miles per hour, and produce sound.

166
00:19:02,510 --> 00:19:03,510
Like radar dishes, our ears channel the sound waves deep into our skulls.

167
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:12,190
Our ear drums vibrate in tune to the frequencies,

168
00:19:12,520 --> 00:19:16,530
moving three tiny bones, each about as long as a grain of rice.

169
00:19:21,200 --> 00:19:22,530
Magnified 20 times, we can see the ear.

170
00:19:27,870 --> 00:19:30,540
Inside, the bones' movements get converted to pulses of pressure,

171
00:19:44,220 --> 00:19:53,230
which vibrates fluid, which disturbs tiny hairs, which excite nerve cells, which translate all of this to the brain.

172
00:19:58,900 --> 00:20:02,240
One sound, perhaps more than any other, is music to our ears.

173
00:20:02,910 --> 00:20:05,240
The human voice.

174
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:10,580
It's an astonishingly versatile instrument, but it's vulnerable.

175
00:20:15,580 --> 00:20:21,260
As lead singer for Aerosmith, Stephen Tyler's work depends on his vocal cords.

176
00:20:30,270 --> 00:20:34,270
Few of us think about the trauma we generate in our voice boxes when we talk, sing, laugh, or scream.

177
00:20:37,940 --> 00:20:40,280
But if you were to look down the gullet of Stephen Tyler,

178
00:20:42,950 --> 00:20:43,950
it would show why he and millions of others are wreaking havoc on a delicate instrument.

179
00:20:55,620 --> 00:20:56,960
Tonight, as Aerosmith perform, Dr.

180
00:20:57,630 --> 00:20:59,960
Steven Zeitels and his team from Massachusetts General Hospital,

181
00:21:03,630 --> 00:21:04,630
will get a rare treat.

182
00:21:04,970 --> 00:21:07,640
With the help of special monitoring equipment,

183
00:21:08,640 --> 00:21:12,640
they'll see how this famous pair of vocal cords holds up to such extremes.

184
00:21:13,980 --> 00:21:15,310
Doctors, Zeitels, one time for my kids, what are you, what is this monitoring?

185
00:21:18,650 --> 00:21:19,320
What we're going to be doing is looking at the vibrations on the skin of your neck,

186
00:21:25,320 --> 00:21:28,660
which is going to pick up the intensity of your voice. It's going to be picking up the loudness of your voice.

187
